1190 0 obj
<<
/Title (Climate change is not only increasing health risks in Kenya and South Africa; it is exposing how access to healthcare is organised when systems come under pressure. Across flooded informal settlements, overcrowded clinics, and drought-affected regions, the question is no longer simply whether healthcare exists, but who is able to reach it, enter it, and be recognised within it.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 38 0 R
/Prev 662 0 R
/Next 1446 0 R
>>
endobj
1191 0 obj
<<
/Title (Box 1: Interrupted Care Under Funding Constraints)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 38 0 R
/Prev 1447 0 R
/Next 663 0 R
>>
endobj
1192 0 obj
<<
/Title (These dynamics show that climate stress does not simply increase health risks. It exposes how access to healthcare is organised under pressure. Services may exist, but the ability to use them depends on environmental conditions, administrative procedures, and financial capacity.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 663 0 R
>>
endobj
